@incollection{BHUSHAN2026139,
 abstract = {Signal processing is the fundamental key component in the development of robotics and autonomous systems. As these technologies’ advances day by day, there is requirement of advance signal processing methods. This chapter delves in to the principles of signal processing for robotics and autonomous systems, which provides a detailed summary of the concept behind signal processing particularly in the field of autonomous systems and robotics, methods, and applications that motivate further research and development in this field. Starting with an overview of signal processing fundamentals, we emphasize their significance for autonomous systems. This includes an explanation of the various analog and digital signal types that are necessary for these systems, as well as an investigation of basic concepts including signals, noise, and filtering. Additionally, significant techniques such as Fourier Transform, wavelets are discussed, laying the groundwork for issues that are more difficult. Subsequently, the chapter explores into the sensors and data acquisition, elucidating the various types of sensors employed in self-governing systems, such as inertial measurement units (IMUs), RADAR, LiDAR, and cameras. All types of sensors are investigated in detail, with a focus on the challenges associated with interpreting the data that the sensors gather. This section stresses the need of reliable data collecting techniques to ensure accurate and consistent operation of autonomous systems. Following that, the preprocessing techniques are discussed, with an emphasis on noise reduction and signal enhancement. Various methods such as filtering, averaging, and data normalization are investigated to get sensor data ready for further inspection. Understanding the process of transforming raw sensor data into a format that can be utilized by autonomous systems is crucial. In addition, this chapter covers the essential processes of feature extraction and analysis, which enable autonomous systems to understand and react to sensor input. Strategies such as Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and Independent Component Analysis (ICA) are examined, along with time-domain, frequency-domain, and time-frequency domain approaches. The use of machine learning in signal processing is one of the major topics covered in this chapter. The improved capacity of signal processing to extract significant information from complicated signals is one of the main benefits of integrating it with machine learning. The ability of conventional signal processing methods to efficiently extract insightful information from complex data streams may be limited. On the other hand, signal processing in machine learning becomes a potent instrument to interpret complicated signals more accurately and effectively by using machine learning algorithms. These approaches are reviewed along with case studies showing how well-known algorithms like Support Vector Machines (SVM) and neural networks are used in robotics and autonomous systems. Moreover, a detailed analysis is done of sensor fusion, the act of merging data from multiple sensors to produce a more accurate and comprehensive depiction of the environment. The application and analysis of techniques such as the Particle Filter and Kalman Filter illustrates several scenarios of autonomous systems. In order to interpret signals in real time, autonomous systems must employ techniques that enable fast and efficient data analysis. The issues and solutions around real-time processing are covered in this part, emphasizing how important it is to the safe and effective operation of autonomous systems. The chapter concludes with a study of signal processing applications in robotics and autonomous systems. Extensive case studies show how advanced signal processing techniques are used in autonomous vehicles, robotic systems, and other applications, underscoring their impact on the capabilities and performance of these technologies. The chapter concludes by discussing the issues and prospects for signal processing for autonomous systems in the near future. Future trends and emerging technologies are examined, providing insights into this field's next phase of innovation. The conclusion offers a summary of the main ideas and closing reflections on the use of signal processing in robotics and autonomous systems in the future. The goal of this chapter is to give readers a inclusive grasp of the vital role that signal processing plays in the creation and functioning of robots and autonomous systems, allowing them to engage with and make contributions to this quickly developing topic.},
